WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.820
DAN SOUZA: Pesto is a simple,
versatile sauce that's

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:07.929
easy to make in a food processor.

00:00:07.929 --> 00:00:09.720
But many home cooks
who try and make it are

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.910
frustrated when their
bright green sauce quickly

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:15.030
turns black, like this pesto here.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:16.950
Why does basil darken this way?

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:20.640
Well, when basil is cut, oxygen
mixes with chemical compounds

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.280
called polyphenols, which are on
the surface cells of the leaf.

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:27.450
This reaction causes the dark
color and bruised appearance.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.790
The more you chop the
basal, the more polyphenols

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:33.000
you are exposing to the oxygen,
and the darker it will get.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.790
And this is why pesto
can turn almost black.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:38.730
However, basil is so prone
to this enzymatic reaction

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:40.890
that even folding or
rolling the leaves can

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:44.250
break enough vascular tissue and
fibers on the surface of the leaves

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:45.810
to cause discoloration.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.660
And I've got two leaves
here to show the point.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:51.330
This leaf here was folded
up, kind of rolled up a bit.

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:52.260
And this one was not.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.090
As you can see, there's
lots of dark lines

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.610
where that oxidation
has already taken place.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:01.070
Enzymes are biological agents that
increase the rate of chemical change,

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:02.700
and not just in basil.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:05.790
Unlike many foods with strong
flavors, garlic and onion

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:08.550
have almost no aroma in this state.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:11.140
No one cries when they're
shopping for an onion.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:14.570
But slice into that onion, and
the aroma can be overpowering.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:16.560
That's when the tears can flow.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:17.850
So what's happening?

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.850
An enzymatic reaction, similar
to the process that causes

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.620
sliced basil to darken.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:25.260
Before slicing, enzymes
and amino acids are

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:28.590
stored in separate sections of
the garlic and onion's cells.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:32.220
When the cell walls are ruptured by
a knife, the enzymes and amino acids

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.149
react with each other
to create new compounds.

00:01:35.149 --> 00:01:37.690
These compounds are responsible
for the characteristic flavor

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:39.570
of garlic and onions.

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:43.890
In garlic, odorless sulfur-containing
amino acids come into contact

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.260
with an enzyme called alliinase.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.360
The reaction of the
two eventually produces

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.930
a new compound called allicin,
which is responsible for garlic's

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.330
familiar scent and flavor.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.750
The more the garlic is broken
down, the more flavor you get.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:06.310
So for example, minced garlic, or garlic
pressed through a mincer like this,

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:12.430
is going to have a lot more
flavor than a piece of garlic

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:13.820
that I just slice very thin.

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:21.385
In this garlic, I'm breaking
a lot of those cells

00:02:21.385 --> 00:02:24.200
and I'm allowing a lot of
that reaction to take place.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.300
Here, I'm doing much
less cellular damage.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.110
So in onion, the process is
the same, but the compounds

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:32.870
created are called thiosulfinates.

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:35.330
Unlike garlic, onions
also contain an enzyme

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:40.340
called LF synthase, which is responsible
for producing the tear-causing compound

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.780
propanethial-S-oxide.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:46.370
In the test kitchen, we've tried more
than a dozen purported home recipes

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:49.400
for keeping tears at bay,
everything from freezing onions

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.620
to lighting a match
while we're slicing them.

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:54.530
But the best solution is
to wear contact lenses,

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:58.400
or don a pair of ski or swim goggles.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.100
What you need is a physical
barrier to protect your eye.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.730
If you don't wear contact lenses,
your best bet is a pair of goggles.

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:06.920
Looks a little bit funny, but it works.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.920
Also, using a sharp knife
with a really good edge

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.350
will inflict less cellular
damage and can actually

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:15.530
limit production of
propanethial-S-oxide,

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:18.380
so you want to make sure
your knife is sharp.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.740
So we understand how
enzymatic reactions start.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.600
But what about stopping them?

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:27.530
One way to halt the enzymatic reactions
is with the application of heat.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:30.050
Heat deactivates enzymes,
while at the same time

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:34.580
transforms these potent flavor and aroma
compounds into more pleasant forms.

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:38.000
This is what happens when you
cook sliced garlic and onions.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.960
You can also add another
ingredient to stop the reaction.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:44.030
In the test kitchen, we ran a
simple test with minced garlic

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.820
for a basic vinaigrette,
with three parts olive oil

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:48.380
and one part lemon juice.

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:51.350
In one batch, we added one
teaspoon of minced garlic

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:53.000
immediately to the dressing.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.980
That's this batch here.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:59.060
In a second batch, we soak the minced
garlic in lemon juice for 10 minutes.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:01.820
Then we made the dressing with
the garlic and lemon juice.

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:05.510
Finally, in a third batch, we let
the grated garlic sit for 10 minutes

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:07.760
while we prepared the other
ingredients, then finally

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.010
mixed it in with the other ingredients.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:13.400
So while these seem like small
adjustments to the order of operations,

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.440
the flavor differences are huge.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:18.440
The citric acid in the lemon
juice speeds the conversion

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:22.400
of that harsh-tasting allicin to some
mellower compounds, the same ones

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:24.630
that are created when garlic is heated.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:27.296
Soaking the garlic in lemon
juice was a bit more effective

00:04:27.296 --> 00:04:29.670
than putting the garlic directly
in the dressing with all

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:30.860
of the other ingredients.

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:34.110
But either approach is far better than
letting the minced garlic hang out

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:35.250
for 10 minutes.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.820
In that sample, the garlic
flavor was overpowering,

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:39.830
and it made for a pretty bad dressing.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:41.504
So this is a really simple experiment.

00:04:41.504 --> 00:04:42.920
I encourage you to try it at home.

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:46.910
You'd really be able to taste
the differences yourself.

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:49.400
Now, what does all this
have to do with pesto?

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.229
Well, you can use heat or acidity to
keep sliced basil from changing color.

00:04:53.229 --> 00:04:55.520
Now, you can find a lot of
recipes on the internet that

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:59.960
suggest throwing a vitamin C tablet,
otherwise known as ascorbic acid,

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:02.540
into the food processor
with the other ingredients.

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:05.930
I have two bowls of pesto here
that I made two hours ago.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:10.510
The only difference is that this one
has 1,000 milligrams of vitamin C in it.

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:14.750
As you can see, it's bright green,
versus the one that doesn't have any.

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.060
Now unfortunately, you can
really taste the vitamin C

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:19.440
and it makes the pesto
kind of unpleasantly tart,

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:22.240
so this isn't really a great option.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:26.570
But I have a third bowl of pesto
here that I also made two hours ago.

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:30.864
Now as you can see, it's pretty much
the same color as the vitamin C batch.

00:05:30.864 --> 00:05:33.530
Now, I can tell you the big
difference is that it tastes exactly

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:35.330
like the darkened pesto.

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:37.910
And that's because it's made
with the identical ingredients

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:40.830
as the dark sample,
with just one change.

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:43.910
I blanched the basil leaves in
simmering water for 20 seconds

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:46.640
before processing them with
the remaining ingredients.

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:48.980
And as I mentioned
earlier, heat deactivates

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:51.440
the enzymes that react
with oxygen to cause

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:53.290
that dark color in the first place.

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:55.830
And the best part is it's
completely effective.

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:58.610
When we ran this test and
refrigerated the pesto for a week,

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:00.320
it was still brilliant green.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:03.528
The only trouble with this technique is
that you need to bring a pot of water

00:06:03.528 --> 00:06:07.209
to a boil, then need to shock and
blanch the basil in a bowl of ice water.

00:06:07.209 --> 00:06:10.250
So this method makes great sense if
you're turning a bumper crop of basil

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:13.670
from your garden into pesto that
you plan to freeze for many months.

00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:17.032
But it's a lot of work for pesto
you want to toss with pasta tonight,

00:06:17.032 --> 00:06:20.240
so I'm going to show you a simpler way
with the test kitchen's favorite pesto

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:21.790
recipe.

